            "text": "**Project Cedar** is a multi-phase research initiative conducted by the **New York Innovation Center (NYIC)** at the **Federal Reserve Bank of New York**. It was created to explore the technical feasibility, architecture, and potential applications of a wholesale central bank digital currency (wCBDC) using distributed ledger technology (DLT).\n\nBelow are the key facts, objectives, and findings of the project:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Background & Purpose\n* **Lead Entity:** The New York Innovation Center (NYIC) at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York (in partnership with the Bank for International Settlements Innovation Hub).\n* **Focus Area:** High-value, wholesale cross-border and cross-currency transactions, particularly foreign exchange (FX) spot trades.\n* **Core Problem Addressed:** Traditional cross-border payments often rely on complex correspondent banking chains, taking multiple days (often $T+2$) to settle, introducing settlement risk, counterparty exposure, and high operational costs.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Phase I (Released November 2022)\n* **Objective:** Test whether a DLT-based wholesale settlement platform could facilitate fast, simultaneous, and final settlement of cross-currency payments.\n* **Network Design:** A permissioned, private blockchain network running simulated wholesale FX spot transactions between mock financial institutions and central banks.\n* **Key Findings:**\n  * **Sub-15-Second Settlement:** The prototype demonstrated atomic settlement of transactions in under 15 seconds end-to-end, compared to the industry standard of two business days.\n  * **Atomic Settlement (Payment-versus-Payment):** Enabled atomic transactions—meaning both legs of an FX trade settled simultaneously or not at all, mitigating settlement risk.\n  * **High Throughput:** Maintained low latency and high transaction throughput across varied simulated transaction volumes.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Phase II: Joint Experiment with MAS / Project Ubin+ (Released May 2023)\n* **Partnership:** The NYIC partnered with the **Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS)** to integrate Project Cedar with Singapore’s **Project Ubin+**.\n* **Objective:** Test cross-border interoperability between distinct, heterogeneous distributed ledger networks operating under different central banks.\n* **Technology Used:** Explored interoperability mechanisms, primarily **Hashed Time-Lock Contracts (HTLCs)**, to connect different blockchain networks without relying on a common shared ledger or a central intermediary.\n* **Key Findings:**\n  * **Cross-Ledger Atomicity:** Proved that atomic cross-currency settlement can succeed across independent, heterogeneous networks.\n  * **Decentralized Settlement:** Both central bank platforms maintained their sovereignty, operational independence, and internal ledger governance while still executing interconnected trades.\n  * **Reduced Settlement Risk:** Mitigated counterparty and settlement risk by guaranteeing that either both transfers settle or both fail automatically.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Important Policy Caveats\n* **Exploratory Only:** The Federal Reserve has emphasized that Project Cedar is strictly a technical experiment and proof-of-concept.\n* **No Issuance Decision:** It does not indicate an intention, directive, or decision by the Federal Reserve Board to issue a central bank digital currency (CBDC) or adopt any specific financial system architecture.",
